    "narrative": "As we finalize the rotation for today\u2019s slate, **Travis Bazzana** stands as our primary focal point at 2B. With a score of 44.8485, **Travis Bazzana** occupies a marginal role today, primarily hindered by a L/L platoon disadvantage against Kay. While the xERA of 4.51 suggests a favorable matchup, his positioning at the bottom of the order (-3%) limits his counting stat ceiling. We are monitoring the lineup closely; should he move up to the two-hole, we expect his efficiency score to climb toward the strong tier, as his raw contact skills remain elite. Unfortunately, our high-upside speed threats like **Corbin Carroll** and **Jose Caballero** are unavailable today due to team off-days and rest, respectively, which heavily limits our ability to aggressively chase points in the SB category.\n\nOur bench strategy remains conservative given the lack of optimal matchups across the board. The decision to keep **James Wood**, **Josh Naylor**, and the rest of our depth pieces sidelined was dictated by the total absence of their teams from today\u2019s schedule, rendering their projection scores zero. We are particularly cautious regarding the risk of burning game caps on hitters facing top-tier arms, as our current roster distribution\u2014featuring high-OBP assets like **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Seiya Suzuki** alongside power-threats like **Giancarlo Stanton**\u2014is designed to maximize efficiency rather than sheer volume. We will re-evaluate the utility of **Brice Turang**, **Mickey Moniak**, and **Trevor Story** pending official confirmation of their respective lineup placements, as any shift in batting order could push them into a 60+ \"Strong\" start classification. **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Max Muncy**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Moises Ballesteros**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Griffin Conine**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Cole Young** remain on standby to fill any gaps left by last-minute lineup changes or injury reporting.",
